    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-114646",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/1daad2a8baabf38dabf1dfe5f8d7e0edf4f3ac8645145f2d0513fc2c59fc26e0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\text{const.}$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $0$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from  $0$ to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/7bef73fc655d0953a315bdd6f9524d0527e54c65453f3359059df7d43c444de7.png)",
